+# Decision: Concurrency policy for independently edited visits
+
+## Context
+
+A visit is identified by a client-generated UUID and may be edited independently on more than one tablet after each has fetched the same starting state. Because tablets can be offline for up to 48 hours, two corrected copies of one visit can reach the server days apart, and the server cannot treat arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative. The record store must decide what a *current* visit means when such copies arrive — and that meaning sets the submission metadata tablets must carry, the shape of the coordinators' review work, and which visit states may enter the Friday export.
+
+The stakes are scientific. A visit's fields are not independent: an observer may revise a species identification and its cover estimate together, and the two carry one judgment. A policy that combines field edits from different submissions can produce a record no observer ever submitted — crossing a scientific relationship it cannot see — while a policy that sends every concurrent edit to review can bury two coordinators in comparisons they must perform by hand before each Friday export. The choice trades those two failure modes against each other and against how much reconciliation logic the team must build and maintain.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Both options retain every submitted edit and keep original submissions intact — that is a standing invariant of the node (HLD tenet: *do not silently discard a submitted edit*), not a discriminator, so it is background here rather than a gate. The elements below are the ones that actually separate the options.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Scientific-relationship safety** — *major.* Whether the policy can emit a visit record that combines edits across a scientific relationship no single observer submitted. Turns on the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and the non-goal *no automated inference of scientific intent*.
+- **Reconciliation logic to build and maintain** — *major.* How much conflict-resolution machinery the team must author and keep correct over time. Turns on the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*: maintained field-merge rules are the kind of standing obligation that tenet resists.
+- **Review workload and export readiness** — *moderate.* How many visits reach the export automatically versus requiring a coordinator to resolve them before Friday. This criterion cuts *against* the recommendation and is the reason the rejected option stays credible.
+- **Inspectability of the exported value** — *moderate.* How directly an exported value traces back to what a worker (or a coordinator) actually submitted. Turns on the *inspectable path* tenet.
+- **Submission size carried by tablets** — *minor.* What each tablet must store and transmit per edit.
+
+Two latent criteria surfaced and were set aside as non-discriminating: *data loss risk* (both options retain all originals, so neither is favored) and *per-conflict coordinator effort* — folded into review workload below, since the policies differ in the *number* of reviews more than in the effort of a single comparison.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each accepted submission stores a complete visit payload as a new revision. Every submission names the base revision it was edited from. A submission whose base is the current tip extends the line and becomes the new current revision; a submission with any other base is stored as a competing revision, and the visit is unresolved until a coordinator selects a competitor or composes a reconciling revision. The server never reads or combines fields across submissions.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A fixes an observer name; worker B, from the same base, adds a missing species. Both name the same base revision. The two submissions become competing whole-visit revisions, and a coordinator picks one or composes a merged visit by hand — even though the two edits touch unrelated fields.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific-relationship safety — **strong.** The server never composes a record from two submissions; every current value is a whole visit one person submitted or one coordinator deliberately composed.
+- Reconciliation logic — **strong.** Needs only lineage tracking and tip comparison; no per-field merge rules to author or maintain.
+- Review workload and export readiness — **weak.** Any concurrent edit, including edits to entirely unrelated fields, produces a competing revision and holds the visit out of the export until resolved.
+- Inspectability of exported value — **strong.** Each exported value is exactly one revision, with an author or a recorded coordinator resolution behind it.
+- Submission size — **partial.** Each edit carries a full visit payload plus its base revision ID.
+
+**Summary.** Minimal reconciliation machinery and whole-visit provenance, paid for in coordinator review volume.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base state and the fields it changed. When two submissions change disjoint fields, the server combines them automatically into one current record; when they change overlapping fields, only the overlap goes to review. Field-level provenance records which submission each field came from.
+
+**Demonstration.** The same observer-name fix and added-species edit touch disjoint fields, so the server combines them with no review, and the visit is export-ready immediately. Only if both workers had edited the *same* species entry would that field alone go to a coordinator.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific-relationship safety — **weak.** Disjoint-field edits are combined automatically; a revised identification and its cover estimate submitted separately can be merged into a record neither observer submitted, with no signal that a relationship was crossed.
+- Reconciliation logic — **weak.** Requires maintained merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, each a standing correctness obligation.
+- Review workload and export readiness — **strong.** Unrelated edits combine automatically, so far fewer visits reach a coordinator and more are export-ready before Friday.
+- Inspectability of exported value — **partial.** The path is recoverable through field-level provenance, but an exported value may be a composite of several submissions rather than one.
+- Submission size — **partial.** Each edit carries a base snapshot and a structured change set.
+
+**Summary.** Fewer reviews and more auto-ready visits, paid for in maintained merge rules and the risk of silently composing a scientifically incoherent record.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on the two *major* criteria, and on both the alternative is **weak**. Field-aware merge's capacity to auto-combine disjoint fields is exactly what makes it unsafe against scientific relationships (*inspectable path* tenet; *no inference of scientific intent* non-goal): the fields the rules treat as independent are sometimes one scientific judgment, and the merge has no way to know. It also carries the larger standing burden — maintained merge rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries — which the *two-coordinators-without-a-systems-administrator* tenet resists. Whole-visit revisions are **strong** on both: no record is ever composed by the server, and the only machinery is lineage and tip comparison.
+
+The alternative remains genuinely credible on the *moderate* review-workload criterion, where whole-visit revisions are **weak**: because any concurrent edit — even to unrelated fields — produces a competing revision, coordinators face a larger review queue, and some visits that field-aware merge would auto-ready are held out of the Friday export until resolved. That cost is accepted deliberately: a larger review queue is preferred over merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without recognizing the relationship. The decision is taken with eyes open on review workload, not because that criterion was judged unimportant.
+
+**What committing forecloses.** The server will not auto-combine field edits, so the review queue cannot be reduced by automatic merging; relief has to come from the coordinator review interface, not from the concurrency model. Reversing to a merge model later would require adding base snapshots and structured change sets to the submission envelope and field-level provenance to storage — a contract change across tablets and export, not a local one.
+
+**What the downstream work must accommodate.**
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain, per locally held visit, the base revision ID it last downloaded, and submit every edit against that base. It must handle three server outcomes for the worker: the edit extended the visit cleanly, the edit became a competing revision (the visit is now unresolved), or the submission was rejected (unknown/foreign base, or idempotency-key reuse with a changed payload). It does not send structured field changes or base snapshots — a full visit payload plus the base revision ID is the whole envelope.
+- **Review-export** must present an unresolved visit's competing revisions for comparison and drive the resolution operation (select a competitor, or record a coordinator-composed reconciling revision, capturing coordinator identity, contributing revision IDs, and outcome). It must treat only resolved visits — those with a current revision and no pending competing revisions — as eligible for the Friday export, and hold unresolved visits out. Because it carries the review-volume cost of this decision, the comparison interface is where coordinator effort per conflict should be minimized.
+
+These are cascade obligations recorded here because their substance is this node's concurrency choice; tablet-sync and review-export are designed in their own segments against this contract.
